rcles away so the area of a circle  is pi r squared we've got two of those  so it's going to be 2 times pi and you  can see that the radius is half the  diameter that's just going to be 2 so pi  times 2 squared  2 squared is 4  times pi times 2 is 8 pi  so the exact answer will be 32 minus 8  pi  okay another example  that comes up quite a bit i've seen this  on a number of practice tests is where  they ask you to find the perimeter but  it's a complicated shape like this now  we know that the perimeter you just have  to add up you know all the sides so the  distance all the way around the object  but this can be a little bit time  consuming i'll show you a shortcut but  go ahead and pause the video see if you  can do this one on your own  so this distance is eight two this  height is two this length is three  here they're not giving us this uh  height but they're giving us this length  is two this height is two we don't know  this length and they're giving us this  height is two so go ahead and see if we  can solve that  and so this is how we would do it now so  basically a shortcut is what you can do  is you can think of this as a rectangle  okay  like so now what do i mean by this what  i mean is that pretend like these little  segments are actually detached like  they're not actually connected at the  vertices at the corners here so what  that allows us to do is it allows us to  push this segment here to the right push  this segment here to the right push this  segment here to the right so you're with  me we're just moving them to the right  and then with all these horizontal  lengths we can move those up  okay like so  and you can see it's forming a rectangle  now we know the perimeter of a rectangle  is just going to be eight  and eight right  and then we just have to add the two  widths now to find out these widths  let's see we have to add two  plus three is five  plus two is seven  and then this dimension here they  actually didn't give us that's my  mistak